ant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=6.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |6|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(6u+6w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[6uw+3{w}^{2}]_{w=0}^{w=1}\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6u(1-0)+3({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}))\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6u+3)\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6u+3)[v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6u+3)\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12u+6)\\,du \\\\  & =[6{u}^{2}+6u]_{u=0}^{u=1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})+6(1-0) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186374125 "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }