here inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-2w|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-2}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [{w}^{2}]_{w=1}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(2-(-2))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (16-1) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186374186 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5y-3{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2+5+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}3\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =54\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186374193 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n